<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Sacred · c. 850 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Veni Creator Spiritus</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Latin hymn about 1,200 years old that calls on the Holy Spirit, still sung when popes are chosen and kings are crowned.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>Rabanus Maurus (attributed)</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>anonymous (Gregorian chant tune, first known from Kempten Abbey around 1000)</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Latin Christians of western Europe</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>Latin</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Frankish realm (exact place not known); tune first known from Kempten Abbey, present-day Germany</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>9th century (text); tune first known around 1000</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Sacred</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (lyrics attributed to Rabanus Maurus (9th century))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veni-creator-spiritus">Veni Creator Spiritus</a> (&quot;Come, Creator Spirit&quot;) is a Christian hymn in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> believed to have been written by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hrabanus-maurus">Rabanus Maurus</a>, a ninth-century Frankish Benedictine monk, teacher, archbishop and saint. When the Latin text is used, it is normally sung to a Gregorian chant tune first known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kempten">Kempten</a> Abbey around the year 1000. Two forms of the words are in use: the original ninth-century text, kept by some Protestant churches and certain Catholic religious orders, and a classicized revision published in 1632 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-urban-viii">Pope Urban VIII</a>, used in most Catholic parishes.</p><p>As an invocation of the Holy Spirit, the hymn is sung in the Catholic Church at Pentecost, at Terce and Vespers. It also opens solemn occasions: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-cardinals">College of Cardinals</a> sings it as it enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sistine-chapel">Sistine Chapel</a> for a papal conclave, and it is sung at the consecration of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, the ordination of priests, confirmation, the profession of members of religious institutes, the dedication of churches, the opening of synods and councils, the coronation of monarchs and the Red Mass that starts the judicial year. It is traditionally chanted on New Year's Day to obtain a plenary indulgence.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> carried it into other churches and languages. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> made a German paraphrase as a Pentecost chorale, first performed and published in 1524 with a melody derived from the chant; it is still in the Protestant hymnal Evangelisches Gesangbuch. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> the hymn has been part of Anglican liturgies since the ordinal of 1550, in a version attributed to Archbishop <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-cranmer">Thomas Cranmer</a>, his only venture into English verse and the only metrical hymn of the Edwardian liturgy; Cranmer added a stanza on the Last Judgement and strife within Christendom that has no parallel in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. From 1562 John Day printed Cranmer's version at the head of the metrical paraphrases in The Whole Booke of Psalmes. More than fifty English translations and paraphrases have been made since the English Reformation.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> John Cosin wrote an English version for the coronation of Charles I in 1625, compressing the seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> verses into four. It entered the 1662 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-common-prayer">Book of Common Prayer</a> and has been sung at every British coronation since, by the choir after the Creed and before the Anointing. At the coronation of Charles III and Camilla in 2023 it was sung for the first time in Irish Gaelic, Scots Gaelic and Welsh as well as English, in new translations by Iain Urchadan, Damian McManus and Grahame Davies. Another English version, by the poet John Dryden, was first published in his Examen Poeticum (1693). In German, Heinrich Bone published a Catholic paraphrase in 1845 and Friedrich Dörr another in 1972; both stand in the Catholic hymnal Gotteslob.</p><p>Composers have set the hymn or its tune for four centuries: Jehan Titelouze (1623), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marc-antoine-charpentier">Marc-Antoine Charpentier</a> (five settings), Johann Pachelbel, Nicolas de Grigny, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/johann-sebastian-bach">Johann Sebastian Bach</a> (the chorale BWV 370 and the organ preludes BWV 631 and 667), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hector-berlioz">Berlioz</a>, Franck, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anton-bruckner">Bruckner</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a>, who set the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> text in Part I of his Symphony No. 8 (1906), Maurice Duruflé, Karol Szymanowski, Paul Hindemith, Krzysztof Penderecki, Karlheinz Stockhausen and Arvo Pärt, among others.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>The hymn is a prayer that calls on the Holy Spirit, as creator, to come to those who sing it. It is an invocation rather than a story: a request for the Spirit's presence and help. That is why it opens solemn acts in which a community asks for guidance, such as conclaves, ordinations and coronations, as well as the feast of Pentecost.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">Latin</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">Latin</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Veni, creátor Spíritus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Come, Creator Spirit,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">mentes tuórum vísita,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>visit the minds of your own,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">imple supérna grátia,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>fill with grace from on high</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">quæ tu creásti péctora.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the hearts which you created.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Qui díceris Paráclitus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You who are called the Paraclete,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">altíssimi donum Dei,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>gift of God most high,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">fons vivus, ignis, cáritas,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>living spring, fire, love,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et spiritális únctio.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and spiritual anointing.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Tu septifórmis múnere,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>You, sevenfold in your gift,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">dígitus patérnæ déxteræ,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>finger of the Father's right hand,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">tu rite promíssum Patris,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>you, duly the Father's promise,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">sermóne ditans gúttura.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>enriching throats with speech.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Accénde lumen sénsibus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Kindle a light for the senses,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">infúnde amórem córdibus,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>pour love into hearts,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">infírma nostri córporis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>the weaknesses of our body</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">virtúte firmans pérpeti.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>strengthening with lasting power.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Hostem repéllas lóngius</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>May you drive the enemy further off</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">pacémque dones prótinus;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and give peace at once;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">ductóre sic te prǽvio</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>with you thus going before as guide</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">vitémus omne nóxium.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>may we shun everything harmful.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Per Te sciámus da Patrem</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Grant that through you we may know the Father</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">noscámus atque Fílium,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and come to know the Son,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">teque utriúsque Spíritum</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and you, the Spirit of both,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">credámus omni témpore.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>may we believe in at all times.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">Deo Patri sit glória,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>To God the Father be glory,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">et Fílio, qui a mórtuis</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>and to the Son, who from the dead</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">surréxit, ac Paráclito,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>rose, and to the Paraclete,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="la">in sæculórum sǽcula. Amen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>for ages of ages. Amen.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Attributed to Rabanus Maurus (9th century); Latin text as in the Vatican's edition. Text from <a href="https://la.wikisource.org/wiki/Veni%2C_Creator_Spiritus_(Libreria_Editrice_Vaticana)" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (8)</summary><ul><li>line 2.1: 'Paraclete' (Greek parakletos) is the Gospel of John's name for the Spirit: one called to stand by, a helper, advocate or comforter. Kept as a name.</li><li>line 3.1: 'septiformis munere', sevenfold in gift: the seven gifts of the Spirit (after Isaiah 11:2-3).</li><li>line 3.2: 'finger of the Father's right hand': the Spirit as God's finger (compare Luke 11:20 with Matthew 12:28).</li><li>line 3.3: 'promissum Patris', the promise of the Father (Acts 1:4); 'rite' means duly, rightly, as is fitting.</li><li>line 3.4: 'sermone ditans guttura', enriching throats with speech: the gift of tongues at Pentecost (Acts 2:4).</li><li>lines 4.3-4.4: 'infirma nostri corporis' is literally 'the weak things of our body'; 'virtute perpeti' is 'with perpetual strength'.</li><li>lines 6.3-6.4: 'credamus' governs 'te': may we believe you (believe in you), the Spirit of both, at all times.</li><li>stanza 7: this doxology names the resurrection ('qui a mortuis surrexit'); other editions close with a different doxology.</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
